Subject: [PATCH 09267/11362] The prompt I wrote told the model the wrong
 engine arity, zeroing every cell that obeyed it

Found by an independent Fable 5 review of the running experiment, verified by
executing the engines on disk.

THE BUG IS MINE, introduced by the 2026-08-15 "tune the prompt" fix. My directive
said:

    def engine(grid, action):            # returns the next grid

Every consumer calls THREE arguments:
  WorldModelVerifier.score            arc_executable_world_model.py:1818
  plan_in_model                       :7749, :7800
  score_goal_predicate_consistency    :2402
and arc_engine_static_validation.py:119-121 states it outright -- "the engine
signature the whole ARC world-model apparatus assumes: engine(grid, action, data)".

WHY IT WAS INVISIBLE. A 2-arg engine loads fine, defines `def engine`, and sets
induce_ok=True. Then score() calls it with three args, catches the TypeError per
transition and SKIPS the row (:1819-1830), so cell_recall is computed over an
empty list and returns 0.0 (:1901). A structurally broken engine is indistinguishable
in the shard from a semantically wrong one.

VERIFIED, not inferred: executed both engines in the run's store.
  sp80 world_model.py -> TypeError: engine() takes 2 positional arguments but 3 were given
  sb26 world_model.py -> runs (it followed the prompt BODY's 3-arg form instead)

And the prompt body ALREADY said three (induce_prompt :3324), so the model was
handed contradictory signatures and each cell resolved it by coin-flip. That is the
entire explanation for the pattern I had been reporting as a Qwen weakness:

  tr87  2/3 induce_ok, cell_recall 0.0 -- obeyed my 2-arg directive
  sp80  2/3 induce_ok, cell_recall 0.0 -- obeyed my 2-arg directive
  sb26  cell_recall 0.6604             -- followed the 3-arg body

I spent several status updates characterising "induce_ok=True with cell_recall 0.0"
as Qwen satisfying the interface and failing the substance. It was satisfying MY
interface, which was wrong.

FIXED: directive now says `def engine(grid, action, data):`, with the reasoning and
the file:line citations inline so it cannot be silently reverted.

ALSO FIXED, same review: `row["budget"]` stamped ARM_BUDGET (32768/16384) while the
actual n_predict was 49152 -- every row's own induce_detail said `HIT n_predict=49152`.
The field exists so a row travelling alone carries the budget that produced it, and
it was recording the opposite.

The 12 contaminated cells are MOVED to contaminated_2arg/, not deleted. The gemma arm
had not started, so pairing is preserved and the re-run is clean.
